---
title: Architecture Overview
description: System overview, package map, runtime components, and the identity/encryption model.
---

`oauth.neoworks.dev` is a Go service (module `github.com/neoworks/auth`) that acts as
NeoWorks' central identity provider, OAuth2/OIDC authorization server, and a
GraphQL API for account-management resources (contacts, OAuth clients,
per-client databases, refresh tokens, media).

## Runtime components

| Component | Tech | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| HTTP server | [chi](https://github.com/go-chi/chi) router | Routes OAuth2/OIDC, REST, and GraphQL traffic |
| Primary database | SurrealDB | Users, OAuth clients, grants, refresh tokens, contacts, media metadata, billing events |
| Cache / ephemeral state | Redis | Authorization codes, login/consent challenges, refresh-token rotation locks, access-token revocation list, device-invite flow, SSO sessions |
| Object storage | MinIO (S3-compatible) | Encrypted media chunks, content-addressed by SHA-256 |
| Signing keys | ECDSA P-256 (ES256) | Access-token signing; exposed via JWKS |

## Process layout (`main.go`)

1. Loads `.env`, sets up structured logging (`logging`/`log.go`).
2. Reads config from environment (`env()` helper with fallbacks) — see
   [development](/docs/api/development#environment-variables) for the full list.
3. Initializes:
   - `crypto.KeyManager` — loads/generates the ECDSA signing key (`KEY_PATH`,
     or `AUTH_PRIVATE_KEY` env var in production).
   - `cache.RedisStore` — Redis connection.
   - `database.NewSurrealStore` — SurrealDB connection + admin credentials
     for per-client database provisioning.
   - `objectstore.New` — MinIO client, ensures the private bucket exists.
4. Builds `oauth.TokenIssuer` (signs/verifies JWTs) and
   `middleware.NewJWTMiddleware` (Bearer-token auth + Redis revocation check).
5. Registers routes on a single chi router (see [http-api](/docs/api/http-api) and
   [graphql-api](/docs/api/graphql-api)):
   - **Public**: `/oauth/authorize`, `/oauth/token`, `/.well-known/jwks.json`,
     `/.well-known/openid-configuration`, key-challenge/device-invite
     endpoints, `/playground`.
   - **GraphQL**: `/graphql` (behind the JWT middleware).
   - **Client-authenticated** (JWT required): `/oauth/introspect`,
     `/oauth/revoke`, `/oauth/userinfo`, authenticated key-management routes,
     all `/api/v1/media/*` routes.
   - **User-facing** (cookie-based, no client JWT): `/auth/login`,
     `/auth/signup`, `/oauth/consent`.
   - **Callbacks** (called by the SvelteKit frontend after login/consent):
     `/oauth/callback/login`, `/oauth/callback/consent`.
6. Starts `http.ListenAndServe`.

## Package map

| Package | Responsibility |
|---|---|
| `main.go` | Wiring/bootstrap |
| `config` | (placeholder — currently empty) |
| `crypto` | ECDSA key management, JWKS generation |
| `oauth` | Domain types (`Client`, `AuthCode`, `RefreshToken`, `Grant`, ...), `TokenIssuer` (JWT mint/verify), PKCE verification |
| `middleware` | JWT auth middleware + request-context claim helpers |
| `handlers` | Top-level OIDC endpoints (JWKS, introspect, revoke, userinfo, consent) |
| `handlers/oauth` | `/oauth/authorize`, `/oauth/token`, `/oauth/callback/*` |
| `handlers/auth` | Login/signup HTML pages + POST handlers |
| `handlers/keys` | End-to-end-encryption key material & device management API |
| `handlers/media` | Chunked, deduplicated, encrypted media upload/download/delete |
| `handlers/shared` | Small shared helpers (error responses, scope parsing, redirect building, ID helpers) |
| `gql` | Generated gqlgen schema/executable code + resolver wiring |
| `gql/resolvers` | Hand-written resolver implementations (Clients, ClientDatabases, Contacts, RefreshTokens) |
| `schema` | `*.graphql` SDL files + `gqlgen.yml` |
| `storage/database` | `SurrealStore` — all SurrealDB queries (users, clients, contacts, media, billing, client databases) |
| `storage/cache` | `RedisStore` — codes, challenges, locks, revocation, device invites, SSO sessions |
| `storage/objectstore` | S3/MinIO chunk storage |
| `sql/migrations` | Versioned `.surql` schema migrations, applied by `cmd/migrate` |
| `cmd/migrate` | Migration runner |
| `tests` | Integration tests + GraphQL test client |

## Identity & encryption model

- Passwords are hashed with bcrypt (`user.password_hash`).
- A per-user **Account Master Key (AMK)** is end-to-end-encrypted client-side:
  - wrapped with a password-derived key (Argon2id, params stored in
    `user_key`) for password-based unlock,
  - wrapped with a recovery key (`recovery_wrapped_amk`),
  - wrapped per-device with that device's public key (`device.wrapped_amk`)
    so new devices can be approved via a QR/device-invite flow without the
    server ever seeing the AMK in plaintext.
- Media chunks are encrypted client-side before upload; the server only
  stores ciphertext, content-addressed by SHA-256, deduplicated per user.

## Versioning pattern

`contact`/`contact_version` and `media`/`media_version` follow a shared
append-only versioning pattern with a generic `derived_from` relation for
ancestry graphs. See [data model](/docs/api/data-model#versioning-pattern) and
the project's `CLAUDE.md` for the canonical write-pattern (transactional
create/update with `derived_from` edges).

## Multi-tenancy: per-client databases

OAuth clients (third-party apps) can provision up to 5 isolated SurrealDB
databases of their own (`client_database` table), with a custom schema
(`schema_def`) applied via generated DDL. Provisioning is billed
(`billing_provisioning_event`). See [data model](/docs/api/data-model) and
[graphql-api](/docs/api/graphql-api#clientdatabases).
